          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Linear type theory
            is the
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Seely89"} Seely 89, prop. 1.5
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Girard87"} Girard 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of/has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+categories"} star-autonomous categories
          %p
            More generally, the term “linear” (as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %em linear type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+languages"} internal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculi
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BlutePanangadenSeely94"} Blute-Panangaden-Seely 94
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Girard87"} Girard 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            means the absence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            map and hence the impossibility of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            to depend on more than a single (linear) copy of their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
          %p
            In the corresponding logical frameworks, the non-cartesianness means one drops the contraction and weakening rules of inference associated with cartesian structure, but retains the exchange rule corresponding to the symmetry constraint. This general type of “linear logic” takes on many flavors: in addition to the Girard-style language that is naturally interpreted in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+categories"} star-autonomous categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} monoidal biclosed categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal closed categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+categories"} compact closed categories
          %p In addition to this “multiplicative” core, one also often studies modalities (comonads) which relate these symmetric monoidal products to cartesian products and coproducts (so-called “additive” operations in linear logic).
          %p
            Extending these considerations, the
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
          %p
            Linear (dependent) type theory can be argued to be the proper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+maps"} diagonal maps
            in its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-cloning+theorem"} no-cloning theorem
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
