          %p (…)
          %h3#TheCanonicalCoModality The canonical co-modality
          %p
            The original axiomatics for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Girard87"}> Girard 87
            ) contain in addition to the structures corresponding to a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"}> star-autonomous
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            a certain (co-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            traditionally denoted “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            ”, the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+modality"} exponential modality
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Benton95"} Benton 95
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Bierman95"} Bierman 95
            = succeed "))" do
              %a{:href => "#Barber97"} Barber 97, p. 21 (26)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for this modality identifies it with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            that is induced from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+adjunction"} strong monoidal adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi R
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi Σ
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Sigma \dashv R)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathcal{C}
                  \\stackrel{\overset{\Sigma}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{R}{\longrightarrow}}
                  S
          %p
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            which interprets the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            If there is only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \;\colon\; S \longrightarrow \mathcal{C}
            without possibly a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Barber97"} Barber 97, p. 21 (27)
            %em
              structural
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fragment"} fragment
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#PontoShulman12"}> Ponto-Shulman 12
            ) it is observed that this in turn is canonically induced if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \simeq \mathcal{C}_\ast
            is the linear type theory over the trivial context of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+closed+monoidal+category"} indexed closed monoidal category
            with category of contexts being
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
