                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/indexed+monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category#TheCanonicalComodality"} indexed monoidal (infinity,1)-category – Exponential modality and Fock spaces
            for more on this.
          %h2#HistoryCategoricalSemantics History of linear categorical semantics
          %p
            There is a long history of logical frameworks of “linear type” and their categorical semantics in various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrines"} doctrines
            of monoidal and closed monoidal categories, even though much of the early history predates Girard’s introduction of linear logic (and which therefore do not bear the term “linear”). We give a thumbnail sketch of some of this below.
          %p
            The founding father could be said to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"} Joachim Lambek
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fragments"} fragments
            of propositional logic. It is noteworthy that one of the various first applications of this categorified cut-elimination was to the (
            %em> linear
            ) doctrine of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+biclosed+categories"} monoidal biclosed categories
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#Lambek58"} Lambek 58
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lambek68"} Lambek 68
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lambek69"} Lambek 69
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Kelly and Mac Lane then adapted the essential cut-elimination techniques of Lambek to study the (difficult) coherence problem for closed symmetric monoidal categories, in their landmark study
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KM71"} KM
              %em is
              made explicit).
          %p
            By the late 1970’s, the connections between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fragments"} fragments
            of classical logic, particularly in sequent calculus form, together with their categorical semantics, was collected in a book-length study (
            %a{:href => "#Szabo78"}> Szabo 78
            ) by Manfred Szabo, who had been Lambek’s student. (While the categorical semantics of various deductive systems were carefully spelled out in Szabo’s book, this work was unfortunately tainted by overly ambitious claims by Szabo regarding his purported solutions to the relevant coherence problems, in terms of normalization algorithms which turned out not to be Church-Rosser; see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Jay90a"} Jay 90a
          %p
            Nor were the logical frameworks restricted to those of sequent calculus type. In particular, the Soviet logician Minc and his students had also developed a type theory whose semantics is naturally valued in closed monoidal categories; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Minc77"} Minc 77
          %p
            Interest in these problems was of course reawakened and reinvigorated in the light of Girard’s extraordinarily insightful 1987 paper
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Girard87"} Linear Logic
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Seely89"} Seely 89
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut-elimination"} cut-elimination
            in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+net"} proof nets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fragment"} fragment
            MLL which is the basic technical core of Girard’s work, was seen as closely related to “extranaturality” graphs (aka KM-graphs) used by Kelly-Mac Lane in their work on the coherence theory for closed symmetric monoidal categories – as well as by other “Australian category theorists” who had developed Kelly’s theory of clubs (which again tended to be most successful for doctrines of linear type).
          %p
            These aspects are covered in (
            %a{:href => "#Blute91"}> Blute 91
            ), who treated a number of coherence problems and particularly the method of Lambek/Kelly-Mac Lane as efficiently streamlined through the method of proof nets with connection given to KM-graphs and clubs. Note that all of those applications were towards various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fragments"} fragments
            of MLL or doctrines of linear type.
          %p
            From approximately the same time period we also have some work of Barry Jay, who had developed a calculus of terms and their normalization to study coherence for closed monoidal categories. See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Jay89"}> Jay 89
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Jay90b"} Jay 90b
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              It should be noted that the “classical” techniques developed by Lambek, Kelly-Mac Lane, and Blute merely yield
              %em partial
              coherence results, in effect evading the notorious “problem of the unit” which had made the full coherence problem for closed monoidal categories seem fearsomely difficult. However, Trimble in his (unpublished) 1994 thesis showed how to re-interpret Girard’s proof nets so that they naturally took the unit into account, and was then able to give a
              %em full
              coherence result for MILL by arranging cut-free nets into a strongly normalizing and confluent rewrite system. Note that while full coherence results for this case had also been proposed by Voreadou and Dole (students of Mac Lane), by Minc and Jay, and also incorrectly by Szabo, none of these earlier approaches were in a position to take advantage of the proof net formalism. For another account of this sort of formalism, specifically for the case of
              = succeed "-autonomous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+distributive+category"} weakly distributive categories
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#BluteCockettSeelyTrimble96"} Blute-Cockett-Seely-Trimble96
          %p
            Meanwhile, the type theory and its categorical semantics for
            %em full
            linear logic, going beyond MLL and taking into account the modalities and the “additive” operations of LL, was also advanced during this time period. A comprehensive description is given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaivaHyland92"} Benton-Bierman-de Paiva-Hyland 92
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#dePaiva89"} de Paiva 89
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HylandPaiva93"} Hyland-de Paiva 93
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bierman95"} Bierman 95
            %a{:href => "#Barber97"} Barber 97
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schalk04"} Schalk 04
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Mellies09"} Melliès 09
          %p Finally, in addition to these usual “denotational” categorical semantics, linear logic also has an “operational” categorical semantics, called the Geometry of Interaction, in traced monoidal categories.
