          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The original article on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            is
          %ul
            %li#Girard87
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear logic
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://girard.perso.math.cnrs.fr/linear.pdf"} pdf
          %p reviewed in:
          %ul
            %li#Girard11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
              , part III of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+Logic"} Lectures on Logic
          %p Linear type theory as such is made explicit in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+John+Ambler"}> Simon John Ambler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em First Order Linear Logic in Symmetric Monoidal Closed Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/92/ECS-LFCS-92-194"} ECS-LFCS-92-194
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/92/ECS-LFCS-92-194/ECS-LFCS-92-194.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Ambler-FOLL.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
            %li
              %p
                Masahito Hasegawa.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Logical predicates for intuitionistic linear type theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~hassei/papers/tlca99.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~fp/courses/15816-f01/handouts/lintt.pdf"} pdf
            %li{:id => "MihályiNovitzká13"}
              %p
                Daniel Mihályi, Valerie Novitzká,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What about Linear Logic in Computer Science?
                %strong 10
                4 (2013) 147-160 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://acta.uni-obuda.hu/Mihalyi_Novitzka_42.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MihalyiNovitzka-LinearLogic.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Wadler"}> Philip Wadler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear Types can Change the World!
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 3
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ioc.ee/ewscs/2010/mycroft/linear-2up.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#lambeks_syntactic_calculus Lambek’s syntactic calculus
          %p
            Several decades before Girard’s article on linear logic, Lambek introduced a sequent calculus without weakening, contraction, or exchange, and described its applications to modeling aspects of natural language (generalizing previous work by Ajdukiewicz and Bar-Hillel on
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorial+grammar"} categorial grammar
          %ul
            %li#Lambek58
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
              \.
              %em The mathematics of sentence structure
              \. American mathematical monthly, 154-170, 1958.
              %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2310058"} link
          %p
            Lambek called his system the “syntactic calculus”, while nowadays it is often called
            %em Lambek calculus
            in linguistics.
          %p
            In “Towards a geometry of interaction” (1989), Girard references Lambek’s 1958 article, and writes that “this work must be acknowledged as a true ancestor to linear logic; its connection to linguistics can be seen as the first serious evidence against the exclusive focus on mathematics” (p.81). On the other hand, Lambek later wrote that his original motivations were actually in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
          %blockquote
            %p
              I would now call this system “bilinear logic”, meaning “non-commutative linear logic” or “logic without Gentzen’s three structural rules”. My original name had been “syntactic calculus”, because of its intended application to linguistics; but actually it had been developed in collaboration with George Findlay [1955] in an attempt to understand some basic homological algebra. Alas, our paper was rejected on the grounds that most of our results were to appear in a book by Cartan and Eilenberg [1956], the publication of which had been delayed because of a paper shortage. (Lambek, “Bilinear logic in algebra and linguistics”,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Advances in Linear Logic
          %h3#categorical_semantics_2 Categorical semantics
          %p The following articles are about “logics of linear type” and their categorical semantics, although they predate the use of the word “linear” in Girard’s sense. Most have a view motivated in part by categorical coherence problems.
          %ul
            %li#Lambek68
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deductive systems and categories
            %li#Lambek69
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deductive systems and categories II
            %li#Minc77
              %p
                G.E. Minc,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Closed categories and the theory of proofs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Selected Papers in Proof Theory
            %li#Szabo78
              %p
                M.E. Szabo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebra of Proofs
            %li#Jay89
              %p
                C.B. Jay,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Languages for monoidal categories
            %li#Jay90a
              %p
                C.B. Jay,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Coherence in category theory and the Church-Rosser property
            %li#Jay90b
              %p
                C.B. Jay,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The structure of free closed categories
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of linear type theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+categories"} star-autonomous categories
            was first described in
          %ul
            %li#Seely89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Linear logic,
                  = succeed "-autonomous" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              %em Contemporary Mathematics
              92, 1989. (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SeelyLinearLogic.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/nets/llsac.ps.gz"} ps.gz
          %p with the first non-syntactic, mathematical categorical model in the same volume:
          %ul
            %li#dePaiva89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"}> Valeria de Paiva
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Dialectica Categories
              %em Contemporary Mathematics
              92, 1989. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vdp/publications/dial87.pdf"}> web
              )
          %p As mentioned above, generalization of this semantics, restricted to the “multiplicative fragment” but covering doctrines of more general symmetric monoidal closed type (with specific reference to Girard’s work) is given in
          %ul
            %li#Blute91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Blute"}> Richard Blute
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear logic, coherence, and dinaturality
          %p
            Blute’s thesis derives coherence theorems for various doctrines of “linear type”, including a coherence theorem for symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+categories"} closed categories
            due to Kelly and Mac Lane:
          %ul
            %li#KM71
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"} Saunders MacLane
              = succeed "," do
                %em Coherence in closed categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404971900132"} web
          %p An decent review of some of this is in
          %ul
            %li#BlutePanangadenSeely94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Blute"}> Richard Blute
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prakash+Panangaden"} Prakash Panangaden
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"} R. A. G. Seely
              %em Fock Space: A Model of Linear Exponential Types
              (1994) (
              %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.27.6825"}> web
              )
          %p Extensions of the coherence-theoretic applications of proof nets in Blute’s thesis, to give full solutions to coherence problems, were given in
          %ul
            %li#Trimble94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd Trimble
                ,
                %em Linear Logic, Bimodules, and Full Coherence for Autonomous Categories, Rutgers 1994
            %li#BluteCockettSeelyTrimble96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Blute"}> Richard Blute
                , Cockett,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"} R. A. G. Seely
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Natural deduction and coherence for weakly distributive categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/002240499500159X"} web
          %p Type theory for full linear logic, together with its categorical interpretation, was developed in
          %ul
            %li#BentonBiermanPaivaHyland92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Benton"}> Nick Benton
                , Gavin Bierman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"} Martin Hyland
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Term assignments for intuitionistic linear logic
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.31.8666"} citeseer
            %li#HylandPaiva93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
                %em Full Intuitionistic Linear Logic
                (extended abstract). Annals of Pure and Applied Logic, 64(3), pp.273-291, 1993. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vdp/publications/fill.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Barber97
              %p
                Andrew Graham Barber,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear Type Theories, Semantics and Action Calculi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/97/ECS-LFCS-97-371/&#8206;"} web
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/97/ECS-LFCS-97-371/ECS-LFCS-97-371.pdf"} pdf
          %p Further review and discussion is in
          %ul
            %li#Bierman95
              Gavin M. Bierman,
              %em
                What is a categorical model of intuitionistic linear logic? (
                %a{:href => "https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.31.8687&rep=rep1&type=pdf"}> web
                )
          %p
            The more general case of general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            is reviewed and further discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Schalk04
              %p
                Andrea Schalk,
                %em What is a categorical model for linear logic?
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/&#8764;schalk/notes/llmodel.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Blute"}> Richard Blute
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Scott"} Philip Scott
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category theory for linear logicians
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear Logic in Computer Science
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511550850.002"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511550850.002
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.site.uottawa.ca/~phil/papers/catsurv.web.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Mellies09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"} Paul-André Melliès
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorial Semantics of Linear Logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Interactive models of computation and program behaviour
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.pps.univ-paris-diderot.fr/~mellies/papers/panorama.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The interpretation of Girard’s
            = succeed "-modality" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            induced from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+adjunction"} monoidal adjunction
            between the closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Bierman95
              %p
                G. Bierman,
                %em On Intuitionistic Linear Logic
                PhD thesis, Computing Laboratory, University of Cambridge, 1995 (
                %a{:href => "http://research.microsoft.com/~gmb/papers/thesis.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Benton95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Benton"}> Nick Benton
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A mixed linear and non-linear logic: Proofs, terms and models
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computer Science Logic. CSL 1994
                %strong 933
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0022251"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0022251
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BentonLinearLogic.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Interpretation of this in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#PontoShulman12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kate+Ponto"}> Kate Ponto
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Duality and traces for indexed monoidal categories
              %strong 26
              23 (2012) 582-659 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1555"}> arXiv:1211.1555
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/volumes/26/23/26-23abs.html"} tac:26-23
          %p Further discussion of linear type theory is for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Chapter 7, Linear type theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~fp/courses/linear/handouts/lintt.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Anders Schack-Nielsen, Carsten Schürmann,
                %em Linear contextual modal type theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.itu.dk/~carsten/papers/lcmtt.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Bernardo Toninho, Luís Caires, Frank Pfenning,
                %em Dependent Session Types via Intuitionistic Linear Type Theory
                %a{:href => "http://ctp.di.fct.unl.pt/~lcaires/papers/dstypes.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Iliano Cervesato,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Pfenning"} Frank Pfenning
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Linear Logical Framework
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.21.1152"} web
          %p
            Discussion of application of linear logic to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            and generally to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Pratt92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Pratt"}> Vaughan Pratt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear logic for generalized quantum mechanics
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings/phycmp/1992/12OmNx19jVS"} Workshop on Physics and Computation (PhysComp’92)
                (1992) 166-180 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.ieeecomputersociety.org/10.1109/PHYCMP.1992.615518"}> doi:10.1109/PHYCMP.1992.615518
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://boole.stanford.edu/pub/ql.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Pratt-LinearLogicForQuantum.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Duncan"}> Ross Duncan
                ,
                %em Types for quantum mechanics
                (2006) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://personal.strath.ac.uk/ross.duncan/papers/rduncan-thesis.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/ross.duncan/talks/2005/pps-22-05-2005.pdf"} pdf
            %li#CCGP09
              %p
                Gianpiero Cattaneo, Maria Luisa Dalla Chiara, Roberto Giuntini and Francesco Paoli, section 9 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Logic and Nonclassical Logics
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Handbook of Quantum Logic and Quantum Structures: Quantum Logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ugo+Dal+Lago"}> Ugo Dal Lago
                , Claudia Faggian,
                %em On Multiplicative Linear Logic, Modality and Quantum Circuits
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1210.0613"}> arXiv:1210.0613
                )
          %p
            Discussion of linear type theory without
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/units"} units
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Houston"}> Robin Houston
              ,
              %em Linear Logic without Units
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2231"}> arXiv:1305.2231
              )
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+types"} inductive types
            in the context of linear type theory is in
          %ul
            %li
              Stéphane Gimenez,
              %em Towards Generic Inductive Constructions in Systems of Nets
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.imn.htwk-leipzig.de/WST2013/papers/paper_16.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
